#
# Lenovo Tab Pen Plus — the stylus's *Bluetooth* side. Not to be confused with
# the drawing digitizer, which is a separate SPI device (`NVTCapacitivePen`,
# VID/PID 0000:0000, INPUT_PROP_DIRECT) that needs no configuration at all.
#
# 17ef:617f enumerates over BT HID as two nodes (measured with `getevent -i`
# on the running stock ROM):
#
#   "Lenovo Tab Pen Plus Consumer Control"  EV=13  KEY=1<<48 (KEY_UNKNOWN=240)
#                                                  MSC=MSC_SCAN
#   "Lenovo Tab Pen Plus Mouse"             EV=17  KEY=BTN_LEFT/RIGHT/MIDDLE
#                                                  REL=X,Y,WHEEL,HWHEEL
#
# The side-button gestures live on the Consumer Control node. Their HID usages
# (consumer page 0x0c, 0x600-0x605) are Lenovo-private, so the kernel HID map
# has no keycode for them and emits KEY_UNKNOWN plus the raw usage in MSC_SCAN.
# That is exactly what the `key usage <code>` form is for — KeyboardInputMapper
# falls back to the usage code when the scan code maps to nothing.
#
# ⚠️ The stock file cannot be copied verbatim. It maps all six usages to Lenovo
# labels — PEN_ONE_CLICK, PEN_TWO_CLICK, PEN_THREE_CLICK, PEN_LONG_CLICK,
# PEN_PRESS_CLICK, PEN_BT_DISCONNECT — none of which exist in AOSP, and
# KeyLayoutMap::load() returns BAD_VALUE and discards the ENTIRE file on the
# first unknown label. One bad label would kill every mapping below it. (Stock
# therefore reaches "this node has no layout" by accident: its own file is
# rejected wholesale on its first line.)
#
# ===========================================================================
# ★★ WHY F13..F18 AND NOT ANYTHING THAT LOOKS MORE SEMANTIC
# ===========================================================================
#
# Three keycode families were considered. Two of them are provably dead ends on
# this ROM, and the reasons are worth keeping because they are not obvious.
#
# 1. STYLUS_BUTTON_PRIMARY / _SECONDARY / _TERTIARY / _TAIL — FORBIDDEN.
#    This file used to do exactly that, and it broke *tapping with the pen*.
#    The chain, every link confirmed on the running device:
#
#    a. EventHub.cpp:2601-2607 — a device that is not TOUCH/TOUCH_MT, is not
#       ALPHAKEY, and has ANY keycode from STYLUS_BUTTON_KEYCODES
#       (KeyCodeClassifications.h:48-53, which is exactly those four) is
#       classified EXTERNAL_STYLUS. The Consumer Control node matches all three
#       tests. Measured: `Classes: KEYBOARD | EXTERNAL_STYLUS | EXTERNAL`.
#    b. That creates an ExternalStylusInputMapper (InputDevice.cpp:622-625) and
#       flips BOTH Novatek nodes to `ExternalStylusPresence: BUTTON_FUSION`
#       (TouchInputMapper::resolveExternalStylusPresence, :338-354).
#    c. TouchButtonAccumulator::processMappedKey (:118-131) returns early when
#       mapKey() fails and never clears mBtnStylus, so the button state LATCHES.
#       Measured: `External Stylus State: Button State: 0x00000020` — a barrel
#       button reported as held for ~50 minutes while nobody touched the device.
#    d. applyExternalStylusButtonState() ORs that bit into every touch frame,
#       and TouchInputMapper.cpp:1487-1491 emits ACTION_BUTTON_RELEASE
#       immediately before ACTION_UP.
#    e. View.java:17011-17015 sets mIgnoreNextUpEvent on ACTION_BUTTON_RELEASE,
#       and :18323 then skips performClickInternal().
#
#    Net effect, and it matched the owner's report word for word: dragging
#    works, tapping intermittently does nothing.
#
#    ⚠️ The lesson is about the *classification side effect*, not the keycode's
#    meaning. Adding a mapping is never "just adding a mapping".
#
# 2. MACRO_1..MACRO_4 — DEAD. PhoneWindowManager.java:5594-5599 clears
#    ACTION_PASS_TO_USER for all four, inside interceptKeyBeforeQueueing (the
#    *queueing* stage). InputDispatcher.cpp:1860-1873 then skips the whole
#    interceptKeyBeforeDispatching stage when POLICY_FLAG_PASS_TO_USER is
#    absent, and that stage is the only place custom input gestures are
#    evaluated (KeyGestureController.java:1099-1113). So a MACRO key can never
#    reach a user-configurable action. An earlier plan in this project was built
#    on the opposite belief; it would have shipped dead keys.
#
# 3. F13..F24 — CORRECT. PhoneWindowManager.java:5600-5618 strips them too, but
#    only `if (!enableNew25q2Keycodes())`, and
#    com.android.hardware.input.enable_new_25q2_keycodes is ENABLED in both
#    release configs this tree can build (build/release/aconfig/{bp2a,
#    trunk_staging}/com.android.hardware.input/…textproto). Verified on the
#    running device rather than inferred:
#        adb shell device_config list input
#          com.android.hardware.input.enable_new_25q2_keycodes=true
#          com.android.hardware.input.enable_customizable_input_gestures=true
#    They pass every other gate too: not isSystemKey(), not isModifierKey(),
#    not in InputGestureManager's blocklist (:79-93), not a system shortcut
#    (:104-378 uses only TAB/SPACE/DEL/ENTER/ESCAPE/DPAD_*/H/I/L/N/S/SLASH),
#    not an app-launch bookmark (bookmarks.xml uses B/P/E/C/M/U/F), and they
#    change no device class (EventHub.cpp:2576-2607: ALPHAKEY needs Q, DPAD
#    needs the DPAD set, GAMEPAD needs the gamepad set, EXTERNAL_STYLUS needs
#    the four STYLUS_BUTTON codes — F-keys are in none of them).
#
# The action each F-key performs is NOT decided here. It is bound at runtime by
# MalbecParts (device/lenovo/malbec/app/MalbecParts) through
# InputManager#addCustomInputGesture, which is per-user, persisted by the
# framework in /data/system/users/<id>/input_gestures.xml, and changeable from
# Settings without a reflash. Keeping the keylayout inert and the meaning
# configurable is the whole point of the split.
#
# ⚠️ Custom input gestures are keyed on (keycode, modifierState) ONLY — they are
# NOT per-device (InputGestureManager.getCustomGestureForKeyEvent, :499-513).
# So the pen and the folio keyboard must not share an F-key. The allocation is:
#     F13..F16  pen buttons: single / double / triple / press-and-hold
#     F17       folio keyboard, touchpad key (F7 keycap)
#     F18       pen, "link going away" — bound by the app, never user-assignable
#     F19, F20  folio keyboard, the two keys right of F12 (App key 1 / 2)
#     F21       folio keyboard, split-screen key (F10 keycap)
#     F22       folio keyboard, PC-link key (F11 keycap)
#     F23       folio keyboard, Fn + App key 1 (search)
#     F24       folio keyboard, full-screen key (F9 keycap)
# ★ THERE IS NO FREE F-KEY LEFT. F24 is the last one and session 21 took it
#   (Vendor_17ef_Product_62b2.kl, usage 0x0c0394). A seventh customisable key
#   would need a different trigger shape — a modifier combination, since
#   InputGestureData triggers are (keycode, modifierState) pairs.
# (⚠️ this table has now been wrong twice. It first read "F13..F18 pen / F19,F20
#  keyboard / F21..F24 free", which stopped being true when session 17's probes
#  moved the folio keys onto F17 and F21..F23; then it read "F24 unallocated —
#  leave free" for two sessions after session 21 allocated it. Keeping it
#  accurate is the entire point of writing it down: the two files' sets must stay
#  disjoint, and they are —
#  pen {F13,F14,F15,F16,F18} vs folio {F17,F19,F20,F21,F22,F23,F24}.)
# Vendor_17ef_Product_62b2.kl's inherited `key 183..194 → F13..F24` block was
# deleted for exactly this reason; see the note there.
#
# ===========================================================================
# The six usages
# ===========================================================================
#
# Names are stock's labels for the same usages
# (work/unpacked/parts/system/usr/keylayout/Vendor_17ef_Product_617f.kl).
# ⚠️ Stock's names are a strong hint about which physical action produces which
# usage, not proof. The gesture → usage mapping has not been measured on this
# unit; the probe is in work/analysis/S16-pen-keyboard-probes.md, and
# MalbecParts labels each button by its stock name, so correcting a swap is a
# one-line change in this file plus a string.
#
#   0x0c0600  PEN_ONE_CLICK      single click of the BT side button
#   0x0c0601  PEN_TWO_CLICK      double click
#   0x0c0602  PEN_THREE_CLICK    triple click (NOT the eraser end — that would
#                                be STYLUS_BUTTON_TAIL, which we must not use)
#   0x0c0603  PEN_LONG_CLICK     press and hold
#   0x0c0604  PEN_PRESS_CLICK    NOT a gesture and DELIBERATELY NOT MAPPED.
#                                It is the "button is being held" state, used by
#                                stock only for hold-the-button-and-tap-the-screen
#                                to start a note. Stock's own dispatcher excludes
#                                it: BluetoothPenInputPolicy.processStylusPenKeyEvent
#                                gives it clickStatusType 4, swallows the DOWN
#                                unless quick-create-note is active, and then
#                                gates the remote-control path on
#                                `clickStatusType != 4`. Stock's settings UI
#                                offers four gestures, not five.
#                                Owner-confirmed on this unit: it fires while the
#                                button is held, so binding it collided with
#                                Press-and-hold. F17 has been given to the folio
#                                keyboard's touchpad key instead.
#   0x0c0605  PEN_BT_DISCONNECT  link-state signal, not an input event
#
# ⚠️ All six share scancode KEY_UNKNOWN(240) and differ only in MSC_SCAN. That
# is fine for KeyboardInputMapper — on key-up it reuses the keycode remembered
# under the scancode (KeyboardInputMapper.cpp:279, :311-325), so a missing
# MSC_SCAN on the up edge still releases correctly — but it does mean two
# gestures cannot OVERLAP: a second DOWN before the first UP is treated as a
# key-repeat of the first keycode (:287-291). Nothing can be done about that
# here; it is a property of the pen's HID report descriptor.
#
# 0x0c0605 is mapped even though it is not a user-facing gesture. Stock consumes
# it in BluetoothPenInputPolicy:853-856 purely to suppress a false anti-loss
# alert when the pen is deliberately powered off, and MalbecParts' anti-loss
# watcher needs the same signal. F18 is therefore RESERVED: MalbecParts binds it
# itself and never offers it in the picker. If the pen turns out never to emit
# it, F18 simply never fires and nothing regresses.

key usage 0x0c0600    F13
key usage 0x0c0601    F14
key usage 0x0c0602    F15
key usage 0x0c0603    F16
key usage 0x0c0605    F18

# ===========================================================================
# Deliberately absent
# ===========================================================================
#
# No `.idc`. The stock one sets `touch.orientationAware` and
# `touch.deviceType = touchScreen`, and both keys are read only by
# TouchInputMapper (TouchInputMapper.cpp:362 and its configureParameters).
# Neither 617f node has any absolute axis, so TouchInputMapper is never
# instantiated for them — the Mouse node is a relative pointer and goes to
# CursorInputMapper, which reads `cursor.orientationAware` instead. The stock
# file is inert on this hardware; carrying it would only add a config whose
# keys nothing reads.
#
# The pen's two REAL barrel buttons are not here either, and must not be: they
# are reported by the DIGITIZER, not over Bluetooth, as BTN_STYLUS /
# BTN_STYLUS2, and Generic.kl:307-308 already maps those to
# STYLUS_BUTTON_PRIMARY / _SECONDARY. The digitizer node is TOUCH-classified so
# it is exempt from the EXTERNAL_STYLUS rule in note 1 above.
